{
  "id": "l09",
  "number": 9,
  "chapter": 2,
  "title": "The Gerund",
  "subtitle": "Having done this, … · the -tvā and -ya endings",
  "tags": [
    "Verb form",
    "Gerund",
    "Absolutive"
  ],
  "minutes": 15,
  "revision": {
    "title": "Quick Revision — Chapter 1",
    "summaryHtml": "You can now build a full Pāli sentence: <strong>Subject (Nom.) + Object (Acc.) + Verb</strong>. Nouns change their endings to show their job, for example <em>naro</em> (subject), <em>naraṃ</em> (object), <em>nagarā</em> (from the city). Verbs end in <em>-ti</em> (one doer) or <em>-nti</em> (many).",
    "connectionHtml": "<strong>Connection:</strong> Until now each sentence had a single action. The gerund lets one person do <em>two things in a row</em>: “having gone, he eats”. It is the first step to longer, more natural Pāli.",
    "questions": [
      {
        "q": "What is the Accusative singular ending of a masculine <em>-a</em> noun?",
        "a": "<strong>-ṃ</strong> (naraṃ)"
      },
      {
        "q": "Which ending of the Ablative means “from” (singular)?",
        "a": "<strong>-ā / -mhā / -smā</strong> (nagarā, nagaramhā)"
      },
      {
        "q": "How do you say “The merchants go”?",
        "a": "<strong>Vāṇijā gacchanti</strong>"
      }
    ]
  },
  "teach": [
    {
      "id": "gerund",
      "title": "The Gerund",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 2,
          "eyebrow": "Grammar — Part A",
          "html": "Doing one thing, <em>then</em> another"
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "html": "The <strong>gerund</strong> (also called the <strong>absolutive</strong>) means “<em>having done</em> …”. It names an action that is finished <strong>before</strong> the main action, and both actions belong to the <strong>same person</strong>."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "html": "The gerund is <strong>indeclinable</strong>: it never changes for number, case or gender. <em>Gantvā</em> means “having gone” whether one person or a hundred went."
        },
        {
          "type": "rule",
          "title": "Forming the gerund",
          "formulas": [
            "root + (<em>i</em>) + <em>-tvā</em> <span style='opacity:.4'>|</span> pac + i + tvā = <em>pacitvā</em> (having cooked)",
            "root + <em>-tvā</em> <span style='opacity:.4'>|</span> gam + tvā = <em>gantvā</em> (having gone)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "caption": "Common gerunds",
          "head": [
            "Verb",
            "Gerund",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "pacati",
              "paci<span class=\"decl-ending\">tvā</span>",
              "having cooked"
            ],
            [
              "khādati",
              "khādi<span class=\"decl-ending\">tvā</span>",
              "having eaten"
            ],
            [
              "gacchati",
              "gan<span class=\"decl-ending\">tvā</span>",
              "having gone"
            ],
            [
              "hanati",
              "han<span class=\"decl-ending\">tvā</span>",
              "having killed"
            ],
            [
              "pivati",
              "pivi<span class=\"decl-ending\">tvā</span>",
              "having drunk"
            ],
            [
              "passati",
              "dis<span class=\"decl-ending\">vā</span>",
              "having seen (passitvā is rarer)"
            ],
            [
              "dadāti",
              "da<span class=\"decl-ending\">tvā</span> / daditvā",
              "having given"
            ],
            [
              "bhuñjati",
              "bhu<span class=\"decl-ending\">tvā</span> / bhuñjitvā",
              "having eaten"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 2,
          "eyebrow": "Grammar — Part B",
          "html": "The <em>-ya</em> ending"
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "html": "When the verb has a <strong>prefix</strong> (like <em>ā-</em> “towards”), Pāli often uses <em>-ya</em> instead. The sounds sometimes blend, so learn these as ready-made words."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "caption": "Gerunds with a prefix",
          "head": [
            "Verb",
            "Gerund",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "āgacchati",
              "ā<span class=\"decl-ending\">gamma</span> / āgantvā",
              "having come"
            ],
            [
              "ā + dā (take)",
              "ā<span class=\"decl-ending\">dāya</span>",
              "having taken"
            ],
            [
              "āruhati",
              "ā<span class=\"decl-ending\">ruyha</span>",
              "having climbed"
            ],
            [
              "otarati",
              "otaritvā",
              "having descended"
            ],
            [
              "nikkhamati",
              "nikkhamitvā / nikkhamma",
              "having left"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 2,
          "eyebrow": "Sentence building",
          "html": "Where does the gerund <em>go?</em>"
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "html": "The gerund (with its own object) comes <strong>before the main verb</strong>. Build the sentence in order of events:"
        },
        {
          "type": "rule",
          "title": "Pattern",
          "formulas": [
            "Subject + [object + <em>gerund</em>] + object + main verb",
            "Naro <span class=\"pali\">gehaṃ gantvā</span> bhattaṃ bhuñjati."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "html": "<em>Naro gehaṃ gantvā bhattaṃ bhuñjati.</em> = “Having gone home, the man eats rice.” Read left to right and you follow the story in the order it happened."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 3,
          "html": "Common mistakes"
        },
        {
          "type": "mistakes",
          "items": [
            {
              "wrong": "Kassakā gantvā<strong>nti</strong> bhuñjanti.",
              "right": "Kassakā gantvā bhuñjanti.",
              "why": "The gerund never takes a plural ending; only the <em>main</em> verb does."
            },
            {
              "wrong": "Naro bhuñjati gantvā.",
              "right": "Naro gantvā bhuñjati.",
              "why": "The earlier action comes first, before the main verb."
            },
            {
              "wrong": "Naro gantvā, kumāro bhuñjati.",
              "right": "Naro gantvā bhuñjati.",
              "why": "A gerund needs the <em>same</em> subject as the main verb."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
